INSERT INTO lessons (section_id, slug, title, summary, concept, real_world, common_mistakes, best_practices, interview_relevance, duration, code_language, code_example, position) SELECT s.id, 'course-intro', 'Course Introduction', 'Welcome to the Java track — here''s what you''ll learn and how to get the most out of this course', 'Welcome to the Java Programming track! This course is designed to take you from the fundamentals of Java all the way to advanced topics like concurrency, design patterns, and building production-ready applications.

Here''s what you''ll cover:
- **Core Java**: Variables, control flow, methods, and arrays
- **Object-Oriented Programming**: Classes, inheritance, polymorphism, and interfaces
- **Collections Framework**: Lists, sets, maps, and utility classes
- **Exception Handling**: Try-catch, custom exceptions, and best practices
- **Concurrency**: Threads, executors, and synchronization
- **Modern Java**: Streams, lambdas, and functional programming
- **Build Tools & Testing**: Maven, Gradle, JUnit, and Mockito

Each topic includes clear explanations, real-world examples, code snippets, and interview tips.', 'Java powers billions of devices worldwide — from Android apps and enterprise backends to big data systems and financial trading platforms. Learning Java opens doors to some of the most in-demand roles in software engineering.', '["Trying to learn everything at once — follow the course order","Skipping fundamentals and jumping to frameworks","Not writing code alongside the lessons","Ignoring best practices and coding conventions early on"]', '["Follow the curriculum in order for the best learning experience","Code along with every example — don''t just read","Revisit topics you find challenging","Use the practice problems to reinforce each concept"]', 'Java is one of the top languages asked in technical interviews at companies like Google, Amazon, and Microsoft. This course prepares you for both OOP design and coding interview questions.', '5 min', 'java', '// Your first Java program
public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Welcome to the Java track!");
        System.out.println("Let''s start coding.");
    }
}', 0 FROM sections s JOIN tracks t ON t.id = s.track_id WHERE t.slug = 'java' AND s.slug = 'welcome';
INSERT INTO lessons (section_id, slug, title, summary, concept, real_world, common_mistakes, best_practices, interview_relevance, duration, code_language, code_example, position) SELECT s.id, 'java-ecosystem', 'Java Ecosystem Overview', 'Understand the Java platform, JVM, JDK, JRE and the broader ecosystem', 'Java is more than just a programming language — it''s an entire ecosystem built around the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).

Key components:
- **JDK (Java Development Kit)**: The full development toolkit — includes the compiler (javac), debugger, and libraries
- **JRE (Java Runtime Environment)**: Everything needed to run Java applications (JVM + core libraries)
- **JVM (Java Virtual Machine)**: The engine that executes Java bytecode — enables "write once, run anywhere"
- **Bytecode**: Java source code compiles to platform-independent bytecode (.class files), which the JVM interprets

The ecosystem also includes:
- **Build Tools**: Maven, Gradle — manage dependencies and build pipelines
- **Frameworks**: Spring, Spring Boot, Jakarta EE — for building web and enterprise applications
- **Testing**: JUnit, Mockito, TestNG — for unit and integration testing
- **IDE Support**: IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, VS Code — powerful development environments', 'The JVM ecosystem runs some of the world''s largest systems. Netflix, LinkedIn, and Uber use JVM-based technologies extensively. Languages like Kotlin, Scala, and Groovy also run on the JVM, making it a versatile platform.', '["Confusing JDK, JRE, and JVM — they are related but different","Not understanding that Java compiles to bytecode, not native machine code","Ignoring build tools and trying to manage dependencies manually","Choosing an outdated Java version instead of using an LTS release"]', '["Always use an LTS (Long-Term Support) version of Java (e.g., Java 17 or 21)","Learn a build tool early — Maven or Gradle","Understand the compile → bytecode → JVM execution flow","Use a modern IDE like IntelliJ IDEA for productivity"]', 'Interviewers often ask about JVM internals, garbage collection, and the difference between JDK/JRE/JVM. Understanding the ecosystem shows depth beyond just writing code.', '10 min', 'bash', '# Check Java version
java --version

# Compile a Java file to bytecode
javac HelloWorld.java

# Run the compiled bytecode
java HelloWorld

# View bytecode (disassemble)
javap -c HelloWorld.class', 1 FROM sections s JOIN tracks t ON t.id = s.track_id WHERE t.slug = 'java' AND s.slug = 'welcome';
INSERT INTO lessons (section_id, slug, title, summary, concept, real_world, common_mistakes, best_practices, interview_relevance, duration, code_language, code_example, position) SELECT s.id, 'setup-environment', 'Setting Up Your Environment', 'Install Java, configure your IDE, and run your first program', 'Before writing Java code, you need to set up your development environment. Here''s a step-by-step guide:

**1. Install the JDK**
Download and install the latest LTS version of Java (Java 21 recommended) from:
- Oracle JDK: oracle.com/java
- OpenJDK (free): adoptium.net or sdkman.io

**2. Set JAVA_HOME**
Configure the JAVA_HOME environment variable so tools and IDEs can locate your Java installation.

**3. Choose an IDE**
- **IntelliJ IDEA** (recommended): Best-in-class Java support, free Community Edition
- **VS Code**: Lightweight with the "Extension Pack for Java"
- **Eclipse**: Traditional choice, fully featured

**4. Create Your First Project**
Use your IDE to create a new Java project, write a simple main method, and run it.

**5. Install a Build Tool**
- **Maven**: XML-based, widely used in enterprises
- **Gradle**: Groovy/Kotlin DSL, more modern and flexible', 'Professional Java developers use IDEs like IntelliJ IDEA daily. Build tools like Maven and Gradle are essential for any real-world project — they handle dependencies, testing, and deployment automation.', '["Not setting JAVA_HOME correctly, causing ''java not found'' errors","Using a text editor instead of a proper IDE for Java development","Installing a non-LTS Java version for learning","Skipping build tool setup — it becomes harder to add later"]', '["Use SDKMAN (sdkman.io) to manage multiple Java versions easily","Start with IntelliJ IDEA Community Edition — it''s free and powerful","Verify your setup by compiling and running a Hello World program","Set up a simple Maven or Gradle project from day one"]', 'While setup isn''t directly asked in interviews, demonstrating knowledge of build tools, project structure, and environment configuration shows professional maturity.', '12 min', 'bash', '# Install Java with SDKMAN (macOS/Linux)
curl -s "https://get.sdkman.io" | bash
sdk install java 21-tem

# Verify installation
java --version
javac --version

# Create a project with Maven
mvn archetype:generate \
  -DgroupId=com.example \
  -DartifactId=my-first-app \
  -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart

# Run with Maven
cd my-first-app
mvn compile exec:java', 2 FROM sections s JOIN tracks t ON t.id = s.track_id WHERE t.slug = 'java' AND s.slug = 'welcome';
